sec.p:
To account for changes in talazoparib exposure over time due to dose modifications and effect of enzalutamide and its n-desmethyl metabolite on talazoparib exposure over time, time-varying average talazoparib concentration values (Cavg, t) were used in the analysis. The area under concentration time curve up to the time when each safety event occurred in the study (AUC, t) were calculated for each patient. This was enabled by generating the individual concentration time profiles using posthoc parameter estimates obtained from population PK analysis (ref) for each patient and calculating AUC, t as:\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
                                                  \usepackage{amsmath}
                                                  \usepackage{wasysym}
                                                  \usepackage{amsfonts}
                                                  \usepackage{amssymb}
                                                  \usepackage{amsbsy}
                                                  \usepackage{mathrsfs}
                                                  \usepackage{upgreek}
                                                  \setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
                                                  \begin{document}$$\:\frac{\mathrm{d}\mathrm{A}\mathrm{U}\mathrm{C},\mathrm{t}}{\mathrm{d}\mathrm{t}}=\mathrm{C}$$\end{document}

where C is talazoparib concentration. Then Cavg, t was calculated as follows:\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
                                                  \usepackage{amsmath}
                                                  \usepackage{wasysym}
                                                  \usepackage{amsfonts}
                                                  \usepackage{amssymb}
                                                  \usepackage{amsbsy}
                                                  \usepackage{mathrsfs}
                                                  \usepackage{upgreek}
                                                  \setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
                                                  \begin{document}$$\:\mathrm{C}\mathrm{a}\mathrm{v}\mathrm{g},\mathrm{t}=\frac{\mathrm{A}\mathrm{U}\mathrm{C},\mathrm{t}}{\mathrm{t}}$$\end{document}

The Cox proportional hazard (PH) model was used to quantify the effects of talazoparib exposure (Cavg, t) and other baseline covariates on safety endpoints. The model is described below:\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
                                                  \usepackage{amsmath}
                                                  \usepackage{wasysym}
                                                  \usepackage{amsfonts}
                                                  \usepackage{amssymb}
                                                  \usepackage{amsbsy}
                                                  \usepackage{mathrsfs}
                                                  \usepackage{upgreek}
                                                  \setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
                                                  \begin{document}$$\:h\left(t\right)={h}_{0}\left(t\right)\times\:{\mathrm{e}\mathrm{x}\mathrm{p}}^{(\theta\:\:\times\:cov)}$$\end{document}

Where h(t) is the hazard rate at time t, h0(t) represents the background hazard rate, \documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
                                                  \usepackage{amsmath}
                                                  \usepackage{wasysym}
                                                  \usepackage{amsfonts}
                                                  \usepackage{amssymb}
                                                  \usepackage{amsbsy}
                                                  \usepackage{mathrsfs}
                                                  \usepackage{upgreek}
                                                  \setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
                                                  \begin{document}$$\:\theta\:$$\end{document} is the coefficient quantifying the effect of covariate on hazard, Cov is the value of covariate of interest.
